## Secure Implementation

### Tier 1: Critical Fix (5 minutes)

Remove user-controlled configuration loading and replace with a static allowlist of approved configuration files.

```java
package com.example.config;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.context.ApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.context.support.ClassPathXmlApplicationContext;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import java.util.Set;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/config")
public class SecureConfigController {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(SecureConfigController.class);

    // SECURE: Static allowlist of approved configuration files
    private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_CONFIGS = Set.of(
        "config/database.xml",
        "config/messaging.xml",
        "config/cache.xml"
    );

    @PostMapping("/load")
    public ResponseEntity<?> loadConfiguration(@RequestParam String configName) {
        // SECURE: Validate against allowlist - no path traversal possible
        if (!ALLOWED_CONFIGS.contains(configName)) {
            log.warn("Rejected config load attempt: {}", configName);
            return ResponseEntity.status(403).body("Configuration not in approved list");
        }

        try {
            ApplicationContext context = new ClassPathXmlApplicationContext(configName);
            log.info("Loaded approved configuration: {}", configName);
            return ResponseEntity.ok("Configuration loaded: " + configName);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.error("Failed to load approved config {}: {}", configName, e.getMessage());
            return ResponseEntity.status(500).body("Configuration load failed");
        }
    }
}
```

**Why this works:** The attacker cannot specify arbitrary XML paths or URLs. Only pre-approved classpath resources load. No user input reaches `ClassPathXmlApplicationContext` without passing the allowlist check.

### Tier 2: Essential Defenses (15 minutes)

Eliminate runtime XML loading entirely. Use Java-based configuration with compile-time safety.

```java
package com.example.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Profile;

@Configuration
public class SecureAppConfig {

    // SECURE: All bean definitions are compile-time verified
    // No XML parsing, no user input, no remote resource loading

    @Bean
    @Profile("production")
    public DataSourceConfig productionDataSource() {
        return new DataSourceConfig()
            .setUrl(System.getenv("DB_URL"))
            .setUsername(System.getenv("DB_USER"))
            .setPassword(System.getenv("DB_PASS"))
            .setPoolSize(20);
    }

    @Bean
    @Profile("staging")
    public DataSourceConfig stagingDataSource() {
        return new DataSourceConfig()
            .setUrl(System.getenv("STAGING_DB_URL"))
            .setUsername(System.getenv("STAGING_DB_USER"))
            .setPassword(System.getenv("STAGING_DB_PASS"))
            .setPoolSize(5);
    }
}
```

**Spring Security hardening for configuration endpoints:**

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                // Config endpoints require ADMIN role
                .requestMatchers("/api/config/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.enable());
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

### Tier 3: Production Hardening (30 minutes)

**Static analysis enforcement with Semgrep:**

```yaml
# .semgrep/rules/no-xml-context-loading.yml
rules:
  - id: no-dynamic-xml-context
    patterns:
      - pattern: new ClassPathXmlApplicationContext($ARG)
      - pattern-not: new ClassPathXmlApplicationContext("...")
    message: >
      Dynamic XML context loading detected. Use Java @Configuration
      classes instead of XML. Never pass user input to ApplicationContext
      constructors.
    severity: ERROR
    languages: [java]
```

**CI/CD pipeline validation:**

```yaml
# .github/workflows/security-scan.yml
name: Component Security Scan
on: [pull_request]
jobs:
  scan:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - name: Block dynamic XML loading
        run: |
          if grep -rn "ClassPathXmlApplicationContext" src/ --include="*.java" | grep -v "test/"; then
            echo "ERROR: ClassPathXmlApplicationContext found in production code"
            echo "Use @Configuration classes instead of XML context loading"
            exit 1
          fi
      - name: Semgrep scan
        uses: returntocorp/semgrep-action@v1
        with:
          config: .semgrep/rules/
```

**Runtime monitoring for configuration loading anomalies:**

```java
@Component
public class ConfigLoadMonitor {

    private final MeterRegistry meterRegistry;
    private final Counter configLoadAttempts;
    private final Counter configLoadRejections;

    public ConfigLoadMonitor(MeterRegistry meterRegistry) {
        this.meterRegistry = meterRegistry;
        this.configLoadAttempts = Counter.builder("config.load.attempts")
            .description("Total configuration load attempts")
            .register(meterRegistry);
        this.configLoadRejections = Counter.builder("config.load.rejections")
            .description("Rejected configuration load attempts")
            .tag("reason", "not_allowlisted")
            .register(meterRegistry);
    }

    public void recordAttempt(String configName, boolean allowed) {
        configLoadAttempts.increment();
        if (!allowed) {
            configLoadRejections.increment();
        }
    }
}
```

**Key operational practices:**

1. **Eliminate XML configs** - Migrate all XML bean definitions to Java `@Configuration` classes. XML configs are harder to audit and create larger attack surface.

2. **Disable bean definition overriding** - Set `spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding=false` in `application.properties` to prevent malicious beans from replacing legitimate ones.

3. **Lock classpath scanning** - Use explicit `@ComponentScan(basePackages = "com.example")` instead of default scanning to prevent unexpected bean registration.

4. **Audit ApplicationContext usage** - Search codebase for any dynamic `ApplicationContext` instantiation and replace with dependency injection.

5. **Network-level protection** - Block outbound connections from application servers to prevent remote XML loading (`iptables -A OUTPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j DROP` for app containers).

6. **Quarterly security review** - Review all `@Configuration` classes and Spring XML files (if any remain) for unintended bean exposure or privilege escalation paths.
